This library is built against, and contains no file of (but copies parts of
YM/Examples/SU2.lean, listed below), the Wilson Yang–Mills
source distribution (repository yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028, commit
3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464), whose project licence is the Apache
License, Version 2.0, and whose NOTICE supplies its attributions.

Several declarations in this library follow statements or proof text of that
distribution:
  PDTAdapters/Divergence.lean, conformal_divergence: the hypotheses are those
    of the statement of YM.Work.A01.divergence (YM/Work/A01/Conserve.lean),
    in its text, with conformal V in place of killing V; the proof follows
    the proof of that theorem up to its last step;
    divergence_of_conformal_killing has the statement of that theorem.
    cons_head_tail states as a lemma a step that the distribution
    proves inline (YM/Euclid/Comparator.lean).
  PDTAdapters/General.lean (all against YM/Work/A01/Conserve.lean), with
    the moments read in a second table throughout. currentIn: the definition
    of YM.Work.A01.current. smooth_consIn, D_consIn: the statements and
    proofs of YM.Work.A01.smooth_cons and D_cons. conformal_divergenceIn:
    the proof follows the proof of YM.Work.A01.divergence up to its last
    step. killing_divergenceIn has the statement of that theorem.
  PDTAdapters/TraceStep.lean, at_unitIn: the statement of YM.Table.at_unit
    (YM/Euclid/Lift.lean) with the moments read in a second table (the
    hypotheses T.respects S and T.Hierarchy in place of S.Basic); the proof
    follows the proof of that theorem.
  PDTAdapters/TraceStep.lean, selected_axis_shape: the statement, in useAt
    form, and the proof of YM.Trace.Verse.axis_shape
    (YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Day.lean).
  PDTAdapters/TraceStep.lean, selected_dilation_rate and
    selected_dilation_not_conserved: the hypothesis is the text of
    YM/Nonperturbative/HeadlineSpec.lean, lines 93-94 (the field
    shortDistance of TraceExclusions, declared at line 92), with a real
    constant in place of Trace.Verse.award G.
  PDTAdapters/FreeControl.lean, polStress_trace: the statement of
    YM.Trace.trace_free (YM/Trace/FreeScalar.lean), with a different proof.
  PDTAdapters/OnHeadline.lean: each theorem takes as its hypothesis that
    distribution's structure YM.Nonperturbative.Headline.TraceExclusions
    (YM/Nonperturbative/HeadlineSpec.lean). The statement of
    headline_dilation_rate is the text of its field shortDistance (lines
    93-94) with the divergence of the dilation current in place of
    euclidean F s.
  PDTAdapters/OnConstructed.lean, constructed_dilation: the statement is the
    statement text of YangMills.constructed (Construction.lean, lines 15-31)
    with two conjuncts appended, the conclusions of headline_dilation_rate
    and headline_dilation_not_conserved_at for the same witness.
    constructed_of_constructed_dilation has the statement of that theorem,
    written as the type of its application. su2_dilation_not_conserved uses
    YangMills.su2 and YangMills.su2Simple (ConcreteSU2.lean).
    The statements of constructed_dilation_not_conserved and
    su2_dilation_not_conserved begin with the opening text of the statement
    of YangMills.constructed (lines 16 and 18-19), the latter at
    YangMills.su2 and b = 1.
  PDTAdapters/LieDim.lean, in the namespace PDTAdapters.SU2Lie: the private
    abbreviations M, A, B, C and comm, the local macro entry, and the private
    lemmas lies, a_in, b_in, ab, drank, c_in, trzero and express are copies
    of the declarations of the same names in YM/Examples/SU2.lean (statements
    and proofs; SUtwo written for that file's local abbreviation G). gens,
    gens_li, gens_span and sutwo_lieDim are this library's.
    award_eq_three_mul_lieDim_of_casimir_ne_zero and award_eq_three_mul_lieDim
    state, for a YM.Gauge, the value that the distribution proves as the
    private theorem award_eq (TraceBridge.lean, lines 24-33, for g.core of a
    simple GaugeGroup) and inline (YM/TraceExtension/Link/Rad.lean, lines
    98-100); the proof follows the step at Rad.lean.
The other statements and proofs are this library's.
